{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 6860a4301626e2ed07617dba51003788fdffb66d, against canonical round-14 report 1f62532ac4a3a9513c78de2c74e29b23457ca3b6. Worktree clean, whitespace clean.\n\nAll three blockers were contradictions I introduced, not gaps I left, and that distinction is the useful part: two of them were sentences that could not both be true sitting a few hundred lines apart, which no amount of care in the local edit would have caught.\n\nThe root said the first batch entry omits its predecessor and carries root=true, and also that the root is a deterministic object known before any batch. I resolved toward the second and made it executable rather than aspirational: the root is an empty batchless commit computed from the genesis hash with fixed identity and fixed dates, so there is no clock in it. Measured before writing this time — two independently initialised repositories, given only the genesis hash, produce the identical OID, and recomputing in place yields it again. That answers your migration question without a descriptor field: genesis-v0 workrooms derive the root by arithmetic from a hash they already have. The first batch is then an ordinary entry parented on it, so no entry has special-case arity and there is nothing to special-case in a walker.\n\nIt also retires the reachability scan, which I am glad about, because you were right that I specified an unbounded traversal without asking its cost. With a computed root, termination is an equality test against a known OID. A second root stops being something to detect and becomes something that cannot be named: an entry claiming rootness while not being that OID is simply not on the ledger. The hostile case survives, with its assertion changed to \"the walk does not terminate at the computed root\" rather than \"a scan found the hidden predecessor\".\n\nhead=\u003cE\u003e is explicit now. You asked how an auditor derives predecessor E, and the honest answer was that it works for an admitted batch and is impossible for an all-refused one, which has no E parent to derive from — the exact case the field exists for. base chains to the predecessor's head, and the chain terminates at the root, whose head is the genesis commit itself. So the anchor is derived rather than declared, and no descriptor change is needed anywhere.\n\nYour ref-creator probe settles that question and I have taken the honest branch rather than the clever one. Two actors pointing differently-named refs at a third party's candidate, and for-each-ref showing only names, target and the victim's object author, with no reflog either side — that is decisive. The record now carries claimed namespace and ref bytes as untrusted input attributed to nobody. Recording an outcome \"against the actor who created it\" was unimplementable, and deriving an attribution from a ref name would have been worse than having none, because it would look like evidence.\n\nThe alias contradiction is the one I should have seen myself, since both halves were mine. If the intake path is completely determined by genesis, namespace, actor and key digest, then every second ref is noncanonical by construction, and I had written no alias grammar that could make one legitimate — so \"valid alias\" named nothing while a spike case demanded it collapse. Removed. One canonical ref per candidate produces the ordinary line; every other ref pointing at it is a separate noncanonical-ref record that adds no candidate parent and consumes none, so the bijection still holds over ordinary lines alone. The grammar changed with it, from a comma-joined list to one length-prefixed ref per line, which is also stronger against the delimiter case since the bytes recorded are now precisely the ones nobody vouches for.\n\nTwo paragraphs downstream still described alias collapse and the refs list; swept. And spike case 4's cross-actor run is now explicitly forbidden from asserting who created the extra ref — a case that demands the impossible does not stay unsatisfied, it gets made to pass by inventing the missing thing.","body":{"commit":"6860a4301626e2ed07617dba51003788fdffb66d","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}